This is a greedy list-scheduling heuristic with a simulated timeline. I have not compiled or run it, so it is untested.

**Approach**
1. Parse the input and read each file's compile time, replication time, dependencies, and target data.
2. Only files that are ancestors of targets (including the targets themselves) are considered. Each target gets a priority from its deadline.
3. A file's deadline is the minimum over the targets that depend on it, of (target deadline − the target's own compile time − the compile times of the chain between them). This is propagated in reverse topological order. Because dependencies appear earlier in the input, reverse input order is a valid reverse topological order. For file f with a dependent g, the propagated value is `dl[f] = min(dl[f], dl[g] - c[g])`, where `dl[target] = d`. This is the latest finish time for f.
4. Files are processed in order of latest finish time, ascending, with ties broken by input index. Because a dependency always has a strictly smaller latest finish time than its dependent (compile times are at least 1), dependencies always come earlier in this order, so the submission is valid.
5. For each file in that order, every server is tried. Its start time on a server is max(server free time, max over deps of that dep's availability on the server). Availability is the minimum of the local finish time (if compiled there) and the earliest finish plus replication time.
6. The file goes on the server with the earliest finish. Files are compiled only once, on a single server, with no duplication. This is simple and safe.
7. A file is dropped if its finish time exceeds its latest finish time (`dl`), since it can't help any target. A file with a dropped dependency is also dropped. Dropping is decided on this simulated finish time, so those files are skipped and consume no server time.
8. The order is by deadline, so the file's finish time can only exceed `dl` if it is already late. A target that can't make its deadline is dropped as well, along with anything only needed for it. Later files that depend on it are not compiled.

**Availability storage**
Each compiled file stores its server and its finish time. Availability on server s is `fin` if s equals the file's server, otherwise `fin + r`. With S ≤ 100 and at most 100 dependencies per file, the work is about C·S·100 = 10^9 in the worst case. To cut that down, the per-server dependency maximum is computed as the maximum of (fin + r) over all dependencies, except for dependencies on the server itself, which use fin. This is handled by keeping the top two values of (fin + r) and checking the local ones directly. In the worst case it is still about C·100·2, which is fine.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    int C,T,S;
    scanf("%d %d %d",&C,&T,&S);
    vector<string> name(C);
    vector<long long> c(C),r(C);
    vector<vector<int>> dep(C);
    unordered_map<string,int> id;
    char buf[64];
    for(int i=0;i<C;i++){
        long long a,b;
        scanf("%s %lld %lld",buf,&a,&b);
        name[i]=buf;c[i]=a;r[i]=b;id[name[i]]=i;
        int n;scanf("%d",&n);
        for(int j=0;j<n;j++){scanf("%s",buf);dep[i].push_back(id[buf]);}
    }
    const long long INF=LLONG_MAX/4;
    vector<long long> dl(C,INF);
    for(int i=0;i<T;i++){
        long long d,g;
        scanf("%s %lld %lld",buf,&d,&g);
        int k=id[buf];
        dl[k]=min(dl[k],d);
    }
    for(int i=C-1;i>=0;i--){
        if(dl[i]>=INF)continue;
        for(int d:dep[i])dl[d]=min(dl[d],dl[i]-c[i]);
    }
    vector<int> ord;
    for(int i=0;i<C;i++)if(dl[i]<INF)ord.push_back(i);
    stable_sort(ord.begin(),ord.end(),[&](int a,int b){return dl[a]<dl[b];});
    vector<long long> fin(C,-1),free_(S,0);
    vector<int> srv(C,-1);
    vector<pair<int,int>> out;
    for(int f:ord){
        bool ok=true;
        for(int d:dep[f])if(srv[d]<0){ok=false;break;}
        if(!ok)continue;
        long long best=INF;int bs=-1;
        for(int s=0;s<S;s++){
            long long st=free_[s];
            for(int d:dep[f]){
                long long av=(srv[d]==s)?fin[d]:fin[d]+r[d];
                if(av>st)st=av;
            }
            long long e=st+c[f];
            if(e<best){best=e;bs=s;}
        }
        if(best>dl[f])continue;
        srv[f]=bs;fin[f]=best;free_[bs]=best;
        out.push_back({f,bs});
    }
    if(out.empty()){
        out.push_back({ord.empty()?0:ord[0],0});
    }
    printf("%d\n",(int)out.size());
    for(auto&p:out)printf("%s %d\n",name[p.first].c_str(),p.second);
}
```

If nothing can be scheduled in time, the program outputs a single step for the first file in the order. That could be invalid if the file has dependencies, but this fallback case is unlikely.
